Lens\n**Phase 1: The Intent vs. Reality Check** - Type Safety issue\n\n## 
Expected vs. Observed\n| Expected Behavior | Observed Risk |\n|---|---|\n| 
Boolean values (True/False) should be rejected as invalid line numbers | Boolean
values are accepted: True == 1, False == 0 |\n| Line numbers must be integers, 
not booleans | `isinstance(True, int)` returns True, so validation passes |\n| 
Invalid type should return error immediately | Invalid type leads to incorrect 
logic flow |\n\n## Root Cause\nIn Python, `bool` is a subclass of `int` where 
`True == 1` and `False == 0`. The current validation uses `type(start) is not 
int` which correctly rejects booleans, but only for the `start` and `end` 
parameters when both are provided. However, when checking `start > end` or other
comparisons, the boolean values still pass through and cause incorrect 
logic.\n\nThe bug is in the validation logic: it uses `type(start) is not int` 
which correctly rejects booleans, but this check is only performed when both 
`start` and `end` are provided. When only one is provided (partial range), or 
when checking `start > end`, the boolean values can still cause issues.\n\n## 
Fix Plan\n1. Add explicit type checking for boolean values in the validation 
logic\n2. Ensure validation happens before any logic that depends on line 
numbers\n3. Use `type(start) is not int` consistently (not `isinstance(start, 
int)`) to reject booleans\n4. Add validation for partial ranges (when only start
or end is provided)\n\n## Status: IN PROGRESS'})
